  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a simplified view of how drug ideas are narrowed from broad chemical exploration into a final medicine. Overall, it contrasts an early “discovery” phase, where many possible chemical directions are explored around a shared core structure, with a later “development” phase, where one promising candidate is filtered through practical requirements before becoming a manufactured medication.\n\nIn the top schematic, a central chemical core branches into many variants spread across “chemical space,” illustrating how medicinal chemists generate multiple related molecules from one scaffold. These branches lead toward a single potential drug candidate, implying that many structural possibilities are tested before one is advanced. That candidate then moves into development, where selection depends on several visible criteria, including safety, economics, intellectual-property freedom, robustness, quality, and efficiency.\n\nThe lower schematic reframes this as a decision-making workflow. A scattered set of colored dots represents many individual ideas in an ideation stage. These are then grouped by theme into aggregated strategies, reducing complexity. Finally, a further selection step based on critical parameters leaves only one dominant group. The main message is that successful drug creation requires both creative exploration and systematic filtering to converge on a viable, manufacturable product.",
